[Event "KID: Classical"]

{The King's Indian lets White build a big centre, then strikes with ...e5 (or ...c5) and storms the kingside with ...f5–f4–g5. Sharp, double-edged, a favourite of Fischer and Kasparov.}
1. d4 {Centre. The pawn on d4 controls e5.}
1... Nf6 {Flexible. The knight on f6 controls d5 and e4.}
2. c4 {Space. The pawn on c4 controls d5.}
2... g6 {[%csl Gg7] The King's Indian fianchetto.}
3. Nc3 {Develops. The knight on c3 controls d5 and e4.}
3... Bg7 {[%cal Gg7d4] The bishop eyes d4 and the long diagonal.}
4. e4 {The big centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5.}
4... d6 {Controls e5, prepares ...e5. The pawn on d6 controls e5.}
5. Nf3 {The Classical. The knight on f3 controls e5.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. Be2 {Develops.}
6... e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] The thematic strike. The pawn on e5 eyes the pawn on d4.}
7. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7... Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Pressure on d4, forcing a decision. The knight on c6 eyes the pawn on d4.}
8. d5 {Closes the centre; White will attack on the queenside.}
8... Ne7 {Heads for the kingside. The knight on e7 eyes the pawn on d5.}
9. Ne1 {Re-routes to d3 to support c5 and f4.}
 (9. b4 {The Bayonet Attack.}
9... Nh5 {Prepares ...f5 and eyes f4.}
10. Re1 {Prepares Bf1 and keeps e4 solid.}
)
9... Nd7 {Clears the f-pawn.}
10. Nd3 {Supports c5. The knight on d3 eyes the pawn on e5.}
10... f5 {[%cal Gf5e4] The kingside storm begins. The pawn on f5 eyes the pawn on e4.} *

[Event "KID: Sämisch, Four Pawns & Fianchetto"]

{The other main White systems against the King's Indian.}
1. d4 {Centre. The pawn on d4 controls e5.}
1... Nf6 {Flexible. The knight on f6 controls d5 and e4.}
2. c4 {Space. The pawn on c4 controls d5.}
2... g6 {KID.}
3. Nc3 {Develops. The knight on c3 controls d5 and e4.}
 (3. Nf3 {Fianchetto system coming. The knight on f3 controls e5.}
3... Bg7 {Develops.}
4. g3 {Fianchetto.}
4... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
5. Bg2 {Long diagonal.}
5... d6 {Solid. The pawn on d6 controls e5.}
6. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6... Nbd7 {Prepares ...e5. The knight on d7 controls e5.}
7. Nc3 {Develops. The knight on c3 controls d5 and e4.}
7... e5 {Strikes the centre. The pawn on e5 eyes the pawn on d4.}
8. e4 {Classical centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5.}
)
3... Bg7 {Develops.}
4. e4 {Centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5.}
4... d6 {Solid. The pawn on d6 controls e5.}
5. f3 {The Sämisch.}
 (5. f4 {The Four Pawns. The pawn on f4 controls e5.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. Nf3 {Develops. The knight on f3 controls e5.}
6... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the overextended centre. The pawn on c5 eyes the pawn on d4.}
7. d5 {Gains space.}
7... e6 {Opens lines. The pawn on e6 eyes the pawn on d5.}
8. Be2 {Develops.}
8... exd5 {Takes. The pawn on d5 eyes the pawn on c4 and the pawn on e4.}
9. cxd5 {Recaptures.}
)
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. Be3 {Develops.}
6... e5 {Strikes. The pawn on e5 eyes the pawn on d4.}
7. Nge2 {Develops.}
7... c6 {Prepares ...b5 or ...cxd5. The pawn on c6 controls d5.}
8. Qd2 {Prepares O-O-O.}
8... Nbd7 {Develops.} *

[Event "KID vs London"]

{Against the London, Black plays a King's Indian setup with ...c5.}
1. d4 {Centre. The pawn on d4 controls e5.}
1... Nf6 {Flexible. The knight on f6 controls d5 and e4.}
2. Nf3 {Develops. The knight on f3 controls e5.}
2... g6 {KID setup.}
3. Bf4 {The London. The bishop on f4 eyes the pawn on c7 and controls e5.}
3... Bg7 {Develops.}
4. e3 {Solid.}
4... d6 {Prepares ...Nbd7 and ...e5. The pawn on d6 controls e5.}
5. h3 {Keeps the bishop safe from ...Nh5.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. Be2 {Develops.}
6... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the centre. The pawn on c5 eyes the pawn on d4.}
7. c3 {Supports d4.} *
